1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,660
It's three in the
morning and you're awake.

2
00:00:01,660 --> 00:00:02,620
Not sick.

3
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:04,930
Not worried about anything in particular.

4
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:14,290
Just awake, staring at the ceiling, doing the arithmetic — if I fall asleep right now
I still get four hours — which is the exact thought that guarantees you won't.

5
00:00:14,290 --> 00:00:18,700
And somewhere in there is the quiet
fear that something is wrong with you.

6
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:22,950
That normal people sleep through the night,
and you have somehow lost the ability.

7
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:24,380
Here's the thing.

8
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:28,320
For most of human history, waking up in
the middle of the night wasn't the problem.

9
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:29,700
It was the schedule.

10
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:40,350
In the nineteen nineties, a historian called Roger Ekirch was digging through old documents
— court records, diaries, medical books, letters, plays — looking for something completely unrelated.

11
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:43,400
And he kept tripping over
the same odd phrase.

12
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:44,600
First sleep.

13
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:59,350
Somebody would write that a thing happened "after the first sleep." A witness would testify that she got up "between
the first and the second sleep." A doctor would advise taking a particular remedy "at first waking."
He started collecting them.

14
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:01,090
He found hundreds.

15
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:07,410
More than five hundred references, across
centuries, in English, French, Italian, Latin.

16
00:01:07,410 --> 00:01:15,290
Different countries, different classes, different eras, all casually referring to
two sleeps as if everybody obviously knew what that meant.

17
00:01:15,290 --> 00:01:16,970
Because everybody did.

18
00:01:16,970 --> 00:01:22,100
Before artificial light was cheap, an ordinary night
in much of Europe went like this.

19
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:24,320
You went to bed not long after dark.

20
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:26,190
You slept for three or four hours.

21
00:01:26,190 --> 00:01:32,550
Then you woke up — not by accident, not because something disturbed
you, but simply because that was the shape of the night.

22
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:35,450
You were awake for an hour, maybe two.

23
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:37,820
Then you went back to
bed and slept until morning.

24
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:40,080
Nobody wrote about this as a problem.

25
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:44,360
Nobody wrote about it much at all, in the
way that nobody writes down that they breathe.

26
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:50,030
The most striking thing about all five hundred references is
that not one of them bothers to explain it.

27
00:01:50,030 --> 00:01:53,300
So what did people actually do
in the middle of the night?

28
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:54,960
Everything, apparently.

29
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:55,950
They talked.

30
00:01:55,950 --> 00:02:01,800
Couples lying in the dark, in the quietest hour of the
day, with no children awake and no work to do.

31
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:09,650
Physicians of the period specifically recommended that hour for sex, on the grounds
that people were rested and would do a better job of it.

32
00:02:09,650 --> 00:02:11,060
They prayed.

33
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:14,560
There are prayer manuals written
specifically for that gap.

34
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:20,290
They got up and tended the fire, checked on
animals, mended things by the light of the embers.

35
00:02:20,290 --> 00:02:22,920
Some people went and
visited the neighbours.

36
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:29,860
There are court records of people committing perfectly ordinary crimes at
two in the morning and no one finding the hour suspicious.

37
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:34,610
And a lot of them just lay
there in a strange half-awake state, thinking.

38
00:02:34,610 --> 00:02:42,410
Ekirch found that people used that hour to interpret their dreams, because they had just
come out of the deepest stretch of dreaming and it was all still there.

39
00:02:42,410 --> 00:02:45,350
That last detail is worth sitting with.

40
00:02:45,350 --> 00:02:52,920
An entire culture of dream interpretation may exist partly because people used to
wake up in the middle of the night with the dream still warm.

41
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:56,280
We don't remember our dreams as
well as our ancestors did.

42
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:58,470
Not because our dreams are worse.

43
00:02:58,470 --> 00:03:00,230
Because we sleep straight through them.

44
00:03:00,230 --> 00:03:02,230
Now, that's history.

45
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:06,730
Documents can be wrong, and people can
describe things they don't actually do.

46
00:03:06,730 --> 00:03:12,690
So in the early nineteen nineties, a sleep
researcher named Thomas Wehr tried something direct.

47
00:03:12,690 --> 00:03:16,890
He took a group of volunteers and
gave them a long artificial winter night.

48
00:03:16,890 --> 00:03:20,900
Fourteen hours of darkness, every
night, for a month.

49
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:24,840
No lamps, no screens, nothing
to do but lie there.

50
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:31,630
For the first few weeks the volunteers mostly slept
off an accumulated debt — long, heavy nights.

51
00:03:31,630 --> 00:03:33,740
And then something changed.

52
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:35,820
Their sleep split in two.

53
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:43,170
They would fall asleep, sleep for about four hours, wake up for
one to three hours, and then sleep again until the light came.

54
00:03:43,170 --> 00:03:45,630
Nobody instructed them to.

55
00:03:45,630 --> 00:03:48,610
It just settled into that shape
once the night was long enough.

56
00:03:48,610 --> 00:03:53,140
And the hour in between was not like being awake
at three in the morning in your bedroom now.

57
00:03:53,140 --> 00:03:55,580
The volunteers described it as calm.

58
00:03:55,580 --> 00:03:56,530
Quiet.

59
00:03:56,530 --> 00:03:57,880
Almost meditative.

60
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:06,030
Their bodies were releasing elevated levels of prolactin — the same
hormone associated with the still, contented state of an animal resting.

61
00:04:06,030 --> 00:04:07,920
It wasn't insomnia.

62
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:09,500
It was a third thing.

63
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,900
A state most people alive today
have simply never been in.

64
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:18,400
There's a second theory about broken sleep, and
it comes from a completely different direction.

65
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:20,110
Not from history.

66
00:04:20,110 --> 00:04:22,650
From the problem of not being eaten.

67
00:04:22,650 --> 00:04:33,010
If you're a group of humans sleeping on the ground, in the open, surrounded by things with
teeth, then a night where everyone is unconscious at the same time is a spectacularly bad idea.

68
00:04:33,010 --> 00:04:34,780
Somebody should be awake.

69
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:40,550
Researchers tested this with the Hadza,
a hunter-gatherer community in Tanzania.

70
00:04:40,550 --> 00:04:51,340
They tracked the sleep of a group of adults across many nights and a couple of hundred hours of
data, and then asked a simple question: how much of that time was every single person asleep at once?

71
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:54,070
The answer was eighteen minutes.

72
00:04:54,070 --> 00:04:58,760
Out of more than two hundred hours, there were
eighteen minutes where the whole camp was out.

73
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:00,780
And they weren't taking shifts.

74
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:02,800
Nobody was assigned a watch.

75
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:08,040
It happened by itself, because people of
different ages naturally drift to different schedules.

76
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:11,110
Older adults went to bed
early and woke early.

77
00:05:11,110 --> 00:05:13,820
Younger adults stayed up
late and slept in.

78
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:18,080
Which suggests something a bit lovely
about a complaint you've definitely heard.

79
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:22,520
Older people who wake at four in the morning
and can't get back to sleep aren't broken.

80
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:25,930
Teenagers who can't fall asleep
before two aren't lazy.

81
00:05:25,930 --> 00:05:28,510
Between them, they cover the whole night.

82
00:05:28,510 --> 00:05:30,340
That's not a malfunction.

83
00:05:30,340 --> 00:05:31,600
That may be the point.

84
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:37,590
Now I have to be honest with you, because this is
where a lot of videos about this stop, and they shouldn't.

85
00:05:37,590 --> 00:05:41,770
Those same hunter-gatherer studies
found something inconvenient.

86
00:05:41,770 --> 00:05:52,590
The Hadza in Tanzania, the San in Namibia, the Tsimané in Bolivia — three groups
on two continents, living without electric light — do not sleep in two blocks.

87
00:05:52,590 --> 00:05:57,040
They sleep once, for somewhere between
six and seven hours, straight through.

88
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:59,220
Less than the eight we're told to get.

89
00:05:59,220 --> 00:06:02,500
And none of the three groups
has a word for insomnia.

90
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:09,550
So the two-sleep pattern that Ekirch found in all those
documents may not be the ancient human default at all.

91
00:06:09,550 --> 00:06:18,110
It may be something specific to long, cold, dark European winters, where the night
lasts fourteen hours and no human being can sleep for all of it.

92
00:06:18,110 --> 00:06:21,960
Which leaves us with two true
things that don't quite fit together.

93
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:25,080
Pre-industrial Europeans clearly
slept twice.

94
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:27,710
Equatorial hunter-gatherers clearly don't.

95
00:06:27,710 --> 00:06:31,430
The honest read is that there is
no single natural way to sleep.

96
00:06:31,430 --> 00:06:37,340
Sleep bends to the length of the night, the temperature,
the danger, and who else is in the room.

97
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:44,380
What there definitely isn't, anywhere in the historical or anthropological
record, is the thing you've been told is normal.

98
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:47,150
So where did the
eight-hour block come from?

99
00:06:47,150 --> 00:06:48,860
Light, mostly.

100
00:06:48,860 --> 00:06:51,690
Cheap, bright, artificial light.

101
00:06:51,690 --> 00:06:57,150
Street lighting spread through European cities and the night
stopped being a wall you hit at sunset.

102
00:06:57,150 --> 00:07:01,920
Then gas, then electricity, and
bedtime slid later and later.

103
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:09,820
The evening — the whole idea of an evening, as a thing you do
rather than a thing you sleep through — is a fairly recent invention.

104
00:07:09,820 --> 00:07:12,700
And the night got squeezed from both ends.

105
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:17,970
Factory work arrived, with clocks and shifts
and a fixed hour to be somewhere.

106
00:07:17,970 --> 00:07:21,820
Sleep stopped being a natural
shape and became a slot.

107
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:28,610
By the early twentieth century, references to the first and
second sleep had almost completely vanished from the language.

108
00:07:28,610 --> 00:07:32,620
Within a couple of generations, people forgot
it had ever been a thing.

109
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:34,870
And then something worse happened.

110
00:07:34,870 --> 00:07:37,920
The consolidated block didn't
just become normal.

111
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:39,390
It became the standard.

112
00:07:39,390 --> 00:07:42,190
The thing you're supposed to achieve.

113
00:07:42,190 --> 00:07:45,330
Eight hours, uninterrupted, or
you're doing it wrong.

114
00:07:45,330 --> 00:07:51,040
So now, when your body wakes you at three in
the morning, you don't experience it as a quiet hour.

115
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:53,380
You experience it as failure.

116
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:57,930
Which brings us back to you,
lying there, doing the arithmetic.

117
00:07:57,930 --> 00:08:06,830
There is a real thing called sleep-maintenance insomnia and it is genuinely miserable
and worth treating, so this isn't a suggestion that everybody's three a.m.

118
00:08:06,830 --> 00:08:07,720
is fine.

119
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:11,490
If you're exhausted every day, go
and see someone about it.

120
00:08:11,490 --> 00:08:14,470
But there's a piece of
this that's worth knowing anyway.

121
00:08:14,470 --> 00:08:18,050
A lot of what makes
night-waking harmful isn't the waking.

122
00:08:18,050 --> 00:08:20,010
It's the panic about the waking.

123
00:08:20,010 --> 00:08:25,170
The clock-watching, the arithmetic, the
certainty that tomorrow is ruined.

124
00:08:25,170 --> 00:08:33,630
That's the part that dumps stress hormones into a body that was trying to be calm,
and that's the part that keeps you up for two hours instead of twenty minutes.

125
00:08:33,630 --> 00:08:36,890
Your ancestors woke at the
same hour you do.

126
00:08:36,890 --> 00:08:40,340
They just didn't lie there thinking
that something had gone wrong.

127
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:42,370
They thought it was the
middle of the night.

128
00:08:42,370 --> 00:08:43,950
Because it was.

129
00:08:43,950 --> 00:08:50,120
So the next time you find yourself awake at three
in the morning, consider the possibility that nothing is broken.

130
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:52,190
You've just arrived in the gap.

131
00:08:52,190 --> 00:08:58,920
The hour that used to be for talking, and praying, and remembering your
dreams, and keeping half an eye on the dark while everyone else slept.

132
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:00,880
Nobody uses it for that anymore.

133
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:06,160
There's a phone next to you and it's very bright
and it will happily take the hour off your hands.

134
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:07,860
But it's still there.

135
00:09:07,860 --> 00:09:08,940
Every night.

136
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:10,090
Waiting.

137
00:09:10,090 --> 00:09:17,840
And if you're now wondering what else about your body you've been
told is a problem when it isn't...
...that's a question for another video.
